---

Declare the extension entry point at compile time with `phpz.module`:

```zig
const extension = @import("extension_info");

comptime {
    phpz.functions(functions, .{});
    phpz.module(.{
        .name = extension.name,
        .version = extension.version,
        .classes = &.{CounterClass},
    });
}
```

The project chooses the Zig identifiers `functions` and `CounterClass`. The PHP function, method and class names exposed by the bindings must match their stub declarations. For example, the first argument to `phpz.Class` sets the PHP class name for `CounterClass`. See [Class registration options]($link.page('reference/registration/classes').ref('class-options')). The skeleton reads the extension name and version from `extension_info`.

# [Module registration options]($heading.id('module-options'))

Use `phpz.module` to export module symbols for a regular extension. When a custom SAPI needs to register an additional module at PHP startup, use `phpz.initModuleEntry` to create its entry. See [Additional modules for a custom SAPI]($link.ref('module-entry')). Both APIs accept the following compile-time configuration. Only `.name` is required.

| Option | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `.name` | Required | PHP extension name as a zero-terminated string, such as `"my_php_extension"`. |
| `.version` | `"0.0.0"` | Extension version string returned by `phpversion("my_php_extension")` for the example above. |
| `.classes` | `null` | Class wrappers to register in order, such as `&.{CounterClass}`. List parents and interfaces before their dependents. |
| `.resources` | `null` | Resource types to register, such as `&.{ValueResource}`. See the example under [Registration order]($link.ref('registration-order')). |
| `.ini` | `&.{}` | [INI entries]($link.page('reference/runtime/ini')) to register. Entries bound to module globals must use the same wrapper type as `.globals`. |
| `.globals` | `null` | The type returned by `phpz.ModuleGlobals(T)`. Each TSRM thread gets its own instance under ZTS. See [Module globals]($link.page('advanced/globals')). |
| `.observer` | `null` | [Zend observer configuration]($link.page('advanced/observers')). Registration is skipped when the extension is loaded temporarily with `dl()`. |

The five optional [lifecycle hooks]($link.ref('lifecycle-hooks')) all default to `null`. That section lists their phases and signatures.

# [Registration order]($heading.id('registration-order'))

PHP discovers functions through the module's function table. The phpz MINIT wrapper processes these entries in order:

1. The generated module symbol registration function, if present, registers module constants and other symbols.
2. The `.ini` settings.
3. The resource types in `.resources`, in array order.
4. The classes in `.classes`, in array order.
5. The `.observer` configuration, except for modules loaded temporarily with `dl()`.
6. `.module_startup_fn`.

Register each class only once through `.classes`. Resources are registered before classes so constructors and methods can create them. List parents and interfaces before dependent classes.

`.resources` accepts types returned by `phpz.zend.Resource.Typed(T, .{ .name = ..., .destroy = ... })`. Module startup assigns each resource type a process-local ID valid until module shutdown. Request-local resources are released with the request. See [PHP resources]($link.page('reference/types').ref('php-resources')).

```zig
const Value = struct { number: i64 };

fn destroy(value: *Value) void {
    phpz.heap.php_allocator.destroy(value);
}

const ValueResource = phpz.zend.Resource.Typed(Value, .{
    .name = "my_php_extension.value",
    .destroy = destroy,
});

comptime {
    phpz.module(.{
        .name = extension.name,
        .version = extension.version,
        .resources = &.{ValueResource},
        // Other settings, including .classes, omitted
    });
}
```

# [Lifecycle hooks]($heading.id('lifecycle-hooks'))

| Option | Phase | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `module_startup_fn` | MINIT | Initialize module state |
| `module_shutdown_fn` | MSHUTDOWN | Release module resources |
| `request_startup_fn` | RINIT | Reset state for this request |
| `request_shutdown_fn` | RSHUTDOWN | Clean up at request end |
| `info_fn` | MINFO | Display extension information |

The first four hooks have the signature `fn () anyerror!void`. `info_fn` takes a `*phpz.ModuleEntry`. Keep request-allocated PHP values within their request. For per-thread module state, see [Module globals]($link.page('advanced/globals')).

See [Lifetime boundaries]($link.page('reference/types/ownership').ref('lifetime-boundaries')) for cleanup responsibilities across calls, requests, objects and module globals.

# [Additional modules for a custom SAPI]($heading.id('module-entry'))

In `examples/custom_sapi`, the host calls `phpz.initModuleEntry(.{ .name = "custom_sapi_app" })` and stores the entry in the global variable `addon_module`. PHP registers the module when the host calls `try custom_sapi.startup(&addon_module)`. PHP scripts can then call the host-provided function `custom_sapi_greeting()`.

Keep the entry's storage alive through module shutdown. See [Custom SAPI]($link.page('advanced/sapi').ref('lifecycle')) for the startup and shutdown sequence.
